<p>
  "Insulation tester" covers a wider range of instruments than most buyers expect — hand-cranked and motorised, metal and wood and bakelite bodies, single-range and multirange, analog and digital. Picking the right one comes down to four questions: how it's powered, what test voltage and range you need, how many voltage ranges on one instrument, and analog or digital readout. This guide walks through each.
</p>

<h2 id="power-source">Question 1 — Hand-Driven or Motorised?</h2>

<p>
  <strong><a href={u('/products/insulation-testers-hand/')}>Hand-driven</a></strong> insulation testers use a manual hand-crank generator to produce the DC test voltage — no batteries, no mains supply. This makes them inherently field-ready: they work in remote substations, on transmission towers, and at sites with no power supply at all. The trade-off is that sustained high-voltage tests (like a Polarisation Index test that runs for 10 minutes) require continuous cranking at a steady speed.
</p>

<p>
  <strong><a href={u('/products/motor-operated-insulation/')}>Motorised</a></strong> insulation testers use a mains-powered electric motor to drive the generator instead, giving sustained, hands-free output at a constant voltage — essential for long-duration PI and DAR tests on large motors, generators, and transformers where consistent voltage over many minutes matters. The CIE/777 HM (motor-cum-hand-driven) variant covers both: motorised when mains power is available, hand-driven in the field when it isn't.
</p>

