<h2 id="body-type">Question 2 — Which Body Type?</h2>

<p>
  For hand-driven testers, body construction affects both durability and voltage range:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Metal (anti-magnetic aluminium die-cast)</strong> — the CIE/444, rugged and compact, covers ranges up to 5000 V.</li>
  <li><strong>Wood (seasoned teak)</strong> — the CIE/666, covers the full range up to 10,000 V; the wood body provides additional insulation margin at the highest test voltages.</li>
  <li><strong><a href={u('/products/compact-insulation-testers/')}>Bakelite (compact)</a></strong> — the CIE/555, a lighter, more compact instrument for lower-voltage routine testing where portability matters more than covering the widest voltage range.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="single-vs-multirange">Question 3 — Single-Range or Multirange?</h2>

<p>
  A standard CIE/444 or CIE/666 tests at one selected voltage per instrument configuration. If your work regularly spans multiple voltage classes — testing 500 V domestic wiring one day and 5 kV switchgear the next — a <strong>multirange</strong> instrument avoids needing separate testers for each. The <a href={u('/products/multirange-insulation-hand/')}>hand-driven MR series (MR-1 to MR-8)</a> and the <a href={u('/products/multirange-motorised-insulation/')}>motorised MR-A to MR-E series</a> offer a selector switch for multiple voltage/range combinations on one instrument, and many include a built-in continuity range as well — see our guide on <a href={u('/blog/multirange-insulation-testers-explained/')}>multirange insulation testers</a> for how to pick between the configurations.
</p>

<h2 id="analog-vs-digital">Question 4 — Analog or Digital?</h2>

<p>
  Analog cross-coil indicators are simple, rugged, and need no batteries to display a reading (the hand-driven generator directly powers the indicator). <a href={u('/products/digital-insulation-testers/')}>Digital insulation testers</a> — the DIT-2500, DIT-5000, and DIT-5005 — give a direct numerical readout, often with data hold and memory functions, which is easier to log accurately and reduces reading-interpolation error on an analog scale, at the cost of needing a battery or mains supply to operate.
</p>

<Callout type="tip" title="Selecting test voltage is a separate decision from the instrument type">
  Whichever instrument type you choose, the test voltage itself should match the equipment being tested — roughly 500 V–1000 V for equipment up to 440 V rated, and 2500 V–5000 V for medium-voltage (3.3 kV–11 kV) equipment. See <a href={u('/blog/what-is-a-megger/')}>our guide to megger use and recommended test voltages</a> for the full table.
</Callout>

<h2 id="decision-summary">Quick Decision Summary</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Field engineer doing spot checks at remote sites, no power available</strong> → hand-driven, CIE/444 (metal) or CIE/666 (wood, for higher voltage).</li>
  <li><strong>Commissioning team running long PI/DAR tests on large machines</strong> → motorised CIE/777 series, or CIE/777 HM if you also need field/hand-driven flexibility.</li>
  <li><strong>Contractor testing equipment across several voltage classes</strong> → multirange, hand-driven MR series or motorised MR-A to MR-E.</li>
  <li><strong>Lightweight routine testing, portability a priority</strong> → CIE/555 compact bakelite-body tester.</li>
  <li><strong>Need precise numerical logging, less concerned about running without a battery</strong> → digital DIT-2500 / DIT-5000 / DIT-5005.</li>
</ul>

